  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 929 AM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nPersistent southwest flow aloft ahead of the upper-level low in\nthe Pacific, along with the help of several shortwaves rotating\neast toward New Mexico ahead of this low, will advect substantial\nsubtropical moisture (some of which is from Tropical Storm Vance) from\nChihuahua into the region this afternoon, starting out east \nbefore becoming area- wide this evening. PWs approach 1\" in the \neastern Rio Grande Valley, and this moisture, as well as decent \ninstability and slow storm motion, could lead to some flooding. \nThese showers and thunderstorms will continue into the overnight \nhours.\n\nAfter a lull in the rain Friday morning, a shortwave will arrive\nin western New Mexico in the early afternoon hours, bringing\nanother round of showers and thunderstorms to the area from the\nwest to the east. Storms are not expected to form until later in\nthe afternoon on Friday, though an improved shear environment\ncompared to Thursday could be conducive to some strong\nthunderstorms, especially out west. These storms will persist into\nthe overnight hours on Saturday.\n\nHalloween looks to be a treat weather-wise as the shortwave exits\nour region, keeping the CWA dry. The Pacific low makes its arrival\non Sunday, and with it another chance of flooding rains. It looks\nto be a fast-moving system, though the ECMWF has it be somewhat\nslower than the GFS. PWs approach 1\" again, though fast mid-level\nstorm motion winds up to 50 mph may not allow for prolonged rain\nover any specific area. A cold front moves through late Sunday\nnight, bringing temperatures for the start of November to be \nslightly below average.",
